## Recommended Solution: Tagged Pointers (Cleaner)

```c
#include <atomic>
#include <cstdint>

struct Node { 
    int val; 
    Node* next; 
};

struct TaggedPointer {
    Node* ptr;
    uint64_t tag;

    TaggedPointer() : ptr(nullptr), tag(0) {}
    TaggedPointer(Node* p, uint64_t t) : ptr(p), tag(t) {}
};

std::atomic<TaggedPointer> top;

void push(int v) {
    TaggedPointer current = top.load();
    Node* n = new Node{v, current.ptr};

    TaggedPointer new_top(n, current.tag + 1);
    while (!top.compare_exchange_weak(current, new_top)) {
        n->next = current.ptr;
        new_top.ptr = n;
        new_top.tag = current.tag + 1;
    }
}

int pop() {
    TaggedPointer current = top.load();
    while (current.ptr) {
        TaggedPointer new_top(current.ptr->next, current.tag + 1);
        if (top.compare_exchange_weak(current, new_top)) {
            int val = current.ptr->val;
            delete current.ptr;
            return val;
        }
    }
    return -1;
}
```

The tagged pointer solution works because:
1. Each pointer has a tag that increments with each update
2. Even if the same memory address is reused, the tag will be different
3. The `compare_exchange` will fail if the tag doesn't match, preventing the ABA problem
4. No locks are needed - it's purely atomic operations

